(a) Why Composite is a Genuine Part-Whole Tree
The Composite pattern is genuinely implemented because:

Uniform Interface: Both EventGroup (Composite) and EventUnit (Leaf) implement the same EventComponent interface with open(), close(), reportStatus(), and getCapacity().

Recursive Composition: EventGroup contains a vector<EventComponent*> of children, which can be either Leaf or Composite objects. This allows unlimited nesting depth.

Operation Delegation: All composite operations recursively delegate to children:

open() → opens all children

close() → closes all children

reportStatus() → reports status of all children

getCapacity() → aggregates capacity of all children

Tree Structure Mirrors Reality:

text
Heap & Heap Bazaar (EventGroup - Root)
├── Food Quarter (EventGroup)
│   ├── Taco Stand (VendorBooth - Leaf)
│   └── Coffee Corner (FoodStation - Leaf)
├── Artisan Row (EventGroup)
│   ├── Pottery Studio (VendorBooth - Leaf)
│   └── Weaving Workshop (VendorBooth - Leaf)
└── Main Stage (PerformanceStage - Leaf)
Transparent Usage: Clients can treat all components uniformly without knowing if they are working with a Leaf or a Composite.

(b) Why Observer is Required Rather Than Direct Hard-Coded Calls
The Observer pattern is essential for the following reasons:

Dynamic Registration: Not all units need to hear all notices. Units can be attached or detached at runtime based on operational requirements.

Loose Coupling: The NotificationHub and EventGroup subjects do not need to know about every concrete leaf type. Observers register themselves through the attach() method.

One-to-Many Communication: A single notice (e.g., WEATHER_ALERT) must reach many different units simultaneously. The Observer pattern handles this naturally.

Cascading Notifications: EventGroup acts as both Observer (receiving from parent) and Subject (forwarding to children), enabling notifications to flow down the composite tree.

Extensibility: New leaf types can be added without modifying any existing Subject code. They simply implement Observer and register.

Polymorphic Reactions: Different observers react differently to the same notice through the handleNotice() method, without requiring type checking in the Subject.

(c) Who Owns Event Components
Ownership Policy:

EventGroup Owns Children: Each EventGroup contains a vector<EventComponent*> of its children. The EventGroup is responsible for deleting these children in its destructor.

Single Parent Constraint: Each EventComponent has exactly one parent (except the root, which has parent_ = nullptr).

Non-Owning Parent Pointer: The parent_ pointer in EventComponent is a non-owning back-pointer used for tree navigation. It is not used for ownership or deletion.

Root Ownership: The client (typically main.cpp) owns the root EventGroup and is responsible for its deletion, which cascades to all children.

Transfer Ownership: When removeChild() is called, ownership transfers to the caller, who must then delete or re-add the component.

cpp
// Ownership Example
EventGroup* bazaar = new EventGroup("Bazaar", false, true, true);  // Client owns root
EventGroup* foodQuarter = new EventGroup("Food Quarter", false, true, true);
VendorBooth* tacoVendor = new VendorBooth("Taco Stand", 50, "Mexican");

bazaar->addChild(foodQuarter);   // bazaar now owns foodQuarter
foodQuarter->addChild(tacoVendor); // foodQuarter now owns tacoVendor

// On destruction:
delete bazaar;  // Deletes foodQuarter, which deletes tacoVendor
(d) Who Owns or Does Not Own Observer References
Observer Ownership Policy:

Non-Owning References: Both Subject (and its subclasses NotificationHub and EventGroup) store observer references as raw pointers (Observer*). These are non-owning references.

Observers are Owned Elsewhere: Observers (leaves and composites) are owned by the Composite tree. The Subject does NOT delete observers.

Lifetime Guarantee: Observers must remain alive while registered and must detach themselves before destruction. The Subject checks for null pointers during notification.

Detachment on Destruction: When an observer is destroyed, it should call detach() on all Subjects it is registered with to prevent dangling pointers.

Why Non-Owning: This prevents double deletion (the Composite tree already owns the observer) and maintains a clear single ownership hierarchy.

cpp
// Observer Storage (non-owning)
class Subject {
protected:
    std::vector<Observer*> observers_;  // NON-OWNING - don't delete!
};

// Observers owned by Composite tree
class EventGroup : public EventComponent, public Subject, public Observer {
    std::vector<EventComponent*> children_;  // OWNING - deleted in destructor
    // observers_ is inherited from Subject - NON-OWNING
};
(e) Why EventGroup Being Both Subject and Observer is Not Misuse
EventGroup plays three roles simultaneously, which is not pattern misuse for the following reasons:

Different Collaborations at Different Levels:

As Observer: Receives notifications from parent or NotificationHub (above)

As Subject: Forwards notifications to children (below)

Different Purposes:

Observer role: "I need to know about external changes affecting my area"

Subject role: "My children need to know about changes I've become aware of"

Real-World Analogy: A precinct manager BOTH receives instructions from headquarters (Observer) AND relays them to individual vendors (Subject). This is natural and not a pattern violation.

Single Responsibility Principle Still Holds: Each role has a clear, distinct purpose:

Composite: Managing the part-whole hierarchy

Observer (receiving): Getting external notifications

Subject (sending): Distributing notifications to children

Explicitly Required by Specification: The specification states:

"Some event areas receive notifications from above and then notify interested units below. Consequently, an event-area class may need to be both an Observer and a Subject. That is not a mistake: the two roles describe different collaborations."

Enables Cascading: This dual role is what makes the notification cascade work:

text
NotificationHub → EventGroup (as Observer) → EventGroup (as Subject) → Children
(f) Push vs Pull Choice
Choice: PUSH Approach

We have chosen the Push approach for our Observer implementation, where the Subject sends the complete Notice object directly to observers via update(Notice* notice).

Reasoning:

Complete Information: The Notice object contains all necessary information (type, message, severity, details) so observers don't need to query the Subject.

Reduced Coupling: Observers don't need a reference back to the Subject to query for state. They simply react to the information provided.

Performance: Observers receive all information at once without additional round-trip queries.

Simplicity: Observers just handle the notice - they don't need to know how to query the Subject.

Better for Cascading: When EventGroup forwards a notice, it can simply pass the same Notice object down without modification.

Information Transferred in Push:

cpp
// Notice object contains:
// - type: NoticeType (OPEN, CLOSE, WEATHER_ALERT, EVACUATION, SHIFT_CHANGE, MEDICAL_ALERT)
// - message: string (human-readable description)
// - severity: Severity (INFO, WARNING, URGENT, CRITICAL)
// - details: map<string, string> (additional structured data)
(g) Key Design Decisions
1. Parent Pointer in EventComponent:

The parent_ pointer is stored in EventComponent (not just EventGroup) so both composites and leaves can navigate up the tree.

2. Automatic Registration:

When EventGroup::addChild() is called, the child is automatically registered as an observer of the parent. This eliminates manual registration code and ensures the cascade works correctly.

3. Critical Services:

InformationDesk and FirstAidPoint are critical services that remain operational during most alerts. They only close during evacuation.

4. Cascade Control:

EventGroup has a canCascade_ flag that allows disabling the notification cascade for specific groups if needed.

5. Notice Types:

Six notice types are supported: OPEN, CLOSE, WEATHER_ALERT, EVACUATION, SHIFT_CHANGE, MEDICAL_ALERT

6. Severity Levels:

Four severity levels: INFO, WARNING, URGENT, CRITICAL